{"tool_name":"kourami","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"kourami","home":"https://github.com/Kingsford-Group/kourami","license":"BSD-3-Clause","summary":"Graph-guided assembly for HLA alleles.","addition_date":"2018-08-16T07:55:03Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:59:01Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:kourami","credit":[{"email":"carlk@cs.cmu.edu","name":"Carl Kingsford","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/Kingsford-Group/kourami"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Peptide immunogenicity prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0252"}]}],"name":"Kourami","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s13059-018-1388-2","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2018 The Author(s).Accurate typing of human leukocyte antigen (HLA) is important because HLA genes play important roles in immune responses and disease genesis. Previously available computational methods are database-matching approaches and their outputs are inherently limited by the completeness of already known types, making them unsuitable for discovery of novel alleles. We have developed a graph-guided assembly technique for classical HLA genes, which can construct allele sequences given high-coverage whole-genome sequencing data. Our method delivers highly accurate HLA typing, comparable to the current state-of-the-art methods. Using various data, we also demonstrate that our method can type novel alleles.","authors":[{"name":"Kingsford C."},{"name":"Lee H."}],"citationCount":21,"date":"2018-02-07T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Biology","title":"Kourami: Graph-guided assembly for novel human leukocyte antigen allele discovery"}}],"topic":[{"term":"Human biology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2815"},{"term":"Immunoproteins, genes and antigens","uri":"http://edamonto